#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083032 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083232 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a" :knowledge-point/title "Counting the Number of Possible Rational Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "509fc92e-cf26-503c-bb89-8df87012e393" :question/math-academy-id "e-5767" :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x)=6x^4-7x^3+11x-9,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{\\textrm{factor of the constant term}}{\\textrm{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-9,$ and its factors are $\\pm1,$ $\\pm3,$ and $\\pm9.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $6,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 3,$ and $\\pm 6.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{3}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{6}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{9}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{9}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{9}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{9}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 9, & \\pm \\frac{9}{2}, & \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that several potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\; \\pm \\frac13, \\; \\pm \\frac12, \\; \\pm 1, \\; \\pm \\frac32, \\; \\pm 3, \\; \\pm \\frac{9}{2}, \\; \\pm 9.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $16$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm$ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2 \\cdot 8 = 16.$)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083106 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083306 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a75b47ec-8d5a-545e-b915-daca92320322" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "12016ef4-6119-543c-adda-aa30fa7a73ef" :question/math-academy-id "e-7365" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x) = 12x^4+5x^3-6x+15?$\n\nI. $\\dfrac{2}{3}$\n\nII. $\\dfrac{1}{3}$\n\nIII. $-\\dfrac{3}{4}$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{\\textrm{factor of the constant term}}{\\textrm{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $15$ and the leading coefficient is $12.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{\\textrm{factor of }{15}}{\\textrm{factor of }12}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $\\dfrac 23$ does not take the above form. The numerator $(2)$ is not a factor of $15.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083107 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083307 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82ccfef3-569a-5ddf-9a69-cae323a0a312" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "20d00218-7823-5ef5-b196-a02894edc69a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7378" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following could be a root of the polynomial $f(x) = 9x^4-3x^2-7x-6?$\n\nI. $-\\dfrac{2}{3}$\n\nII. $\\dfrac{3}{2}$\n\nIII. $-9$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{\\textrm{factor of the constant term}}{\\textrm{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-6,$ and the leading coefficient is $9.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{\\textrm{factor of }{-6}}{\\textrm{factor of }9}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-\\dfrac{2}{3}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(2)$ is a factor of $-6$ and the denominator $(3)$ is a factor of $9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083110 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "001262b8-82b7-5421-80ac-8608b20d4be1" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "e7c9c0d2-6af5-520a-8b56-4930780bc6df" :question/math-academy-id "e-7413" :question/problem "Given that the polynomial $f(x) =x^3 - x^2 - 4x + 4$ has a root in the interval $(0,4),$ find the sum of the distinct real roots of $f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{\\textrm{factor of the constant term}}{\\textrm{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $4$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\dfrac{\\textrm{factor of }{4}}{\\textrm{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm1, \\pm 2, \\pm 4\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(0,4),$ are $1$ and $2.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $\\left(x-1\\right)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-4$ | $4$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $1$ | $0$ | $-4$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $\\left(x-1\\right)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\left( x - 1\\right) (x^2 - 4).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-1)({x}^{2}-4) \\\\  & =(x-1)(x+2)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=-2, 1, 2.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n-2+1+2 =1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433673 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083307 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82ccfef3-569a-5ddf-9a69-cae323a0a312" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1436fc42-169a-5d6e-8e67-e55cf95c9b4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433635} {:answer/id #uuid "163d085f-a04f-5649-b71b-e90a4a64a76e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433637} {:answer/id #uuid "7b81c397-2f18-5ef5-b521-31182e577fe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433646} {:answer/id #uuid "c244dcdf-d702-5e3f-8cc2-53ceb79a1e2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433654} {:answer/id #uuid "e742a257-c79d-5e7c-946b-347b8a6e55d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e742a257-c79d-5e7c-946b-347b8a6e55d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433663} :answer-field/id #uuid "b65fa8f3-11c1-5f2d-b151-e84007d15bf9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433671}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "879d12d0-2113-5e81-a5d2-2d55d4183634" :question/math-academy-id "q-112864" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following could be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=-4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-3x+2?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $2,$ and the leading coefficient is $-4.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }2}{\\text{factor of}\\,-4}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $\\frac{1}{4}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(1)$ is a factor of $2$ and the denominator $(4)$ is a factor of $-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369147 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083306 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a75b47ec-8d5a-545e-b915-daca92320322" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a0522a7-0507-5731-a4b7-8bba18622019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369149} {:answer/id #uuid "8fde88cc-1dfe-551e-b505-43ea2c33d068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369150} {:answer/id #uuid "83ab7bbf-94b2-5cb5-9325-46ad92883dbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369151} {:answer/id #uuid "a6bc4bf1-1c7f-5632-a344-3a92a856f8ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369152} {:answer/id #uuid "ba1d826c-ea85-5f67-b6de-9779918444e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6bc4bf1-1c7f-5632-a344-3a92a856f8ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369152} :answer-field/id #uuid "70b789b7-b641-5d56-91db-ef1575144f19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ff153540-97c0-598a-84af-1ded375d76ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-112872" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{4}-3{x}^{2}+x+6?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $6$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }6}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $\\frac{3}{4}$ does not take the above form. The denominator $(4)$ is not a factor of $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433675 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "001262b8-82b7-5421-80ac-8608b20d4be1" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "285ade38-532c-552a-8139-54551e46a1fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433639} {:answer/id #uuid "67e7348d-395b-5ab0-8891-b4eb9d1a0356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433644} {:answer/id #uuid "97c4e86d-95a3-53e3-b80a-a14798afdf4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433650} {:answer/id #uuid "9cb2fbb0-cb33-517d-ab50-a44b4aa0e364" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433651} {:answer/id #uuid "d6815b33-3bbe-52e5-a8e1-66282caf5f21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433660}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67e7348d-395b-5ab0-8891-b4eb9d1a0356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433644} :answer-field/id #uuid "91e3cfc1-8fc3-56d2-9a41-2e4cf39a8430" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433670}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b9c7c178-4ebe-5318-8cb7-742204e595a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-112977" :question/problem "Given that the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-3x+18$ has a root in the interval $(2,4),$ find the sum of the **distinct** real roots of $f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $18$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }18}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 9,\\,\\pm 18\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only root in the interval $(2,4)$ is $3.$\n\nUsing the synthetic division, we get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $-3$ | $18$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $-3$ | $-18$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-1$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-3)({x}^{2}-x-6).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-3)({x}^{2}-x-6) \\\\  & =(x-3)(x+2)(x-3) \\\\  & =(x+2)(x-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=-2,3.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n-2+3=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272279 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083232 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a" :knowledge-point/title "Counting the Number of Possible Rational Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0717f98a-a00b-5e12-b164-bbe07b4506d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269334} {:answer/id #uuid "dc0ed403-118c-5e8a-b288-f4a9fdf647c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271195} {:answer/id #uuid "f6b8d9d8-b733-55b7-8453-d96684728e42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271433} {:answer/id #uuid "fa96b2fb-d03d-5d55-ae12-d85627604770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271459} {:answer/id #uuid "fe5ffb9c-df8a-54bf-b04e-3951b89b2186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0717f98a-a00b-5e12-b164-bbe07b4506d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269334} :answer-field/id #uuid "08b318fe-5549-5f3d-b17c-a826bb2d784c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271521}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b0caeff2-4a23-5ec8-b046-51ecdd035f70" :question/math-academy-id "q-14142" :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x)=6{x}^{4}-15{x}^{2}-2x+5,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $5,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 5.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $6,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3$ and $\\pm 6.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{5}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm 5, & \\pm \\frac{5}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that none of these potential roots are duplicates. Listing those potential roots in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{6},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{6},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{2},\\,\\pm 5.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $16$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 8=16.$)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433672 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083232 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a" :knowledge-point/title "Counting the Number of Possible Rational Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "087ad481-79d2-5803-a05b-415265f74035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433634} {:answer/id #uuid "68ce9788-0470-5c3e-a1b2-2e1c81f8f0d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433645} {:answer/id #uuid "cd3b1417-956a-5906-8f67-b13dacbb7ab9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433655} {:answer/id #uuid "cd8039a9-93f2-5720-ab0a-1873075b3cd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433656} {:answer/id #uuid "d1b2ae26-7ce6-5b5d-8c0d-644be4a27793" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "087ad481-79d2-5803-a05b-415265f74035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433634} :answer-field/id #uuid "60877b9d-92c5-51c5-86d7-d416451d8505" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433667}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2e2ad5ba-576c-5412-b8cf-f6ef173b3a57" :question/math-academy-id "q-14143" :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x)=4{x}^{7}-18{x}^{2}+26x-12,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-12,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 6$ and $\\pm 12.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $4,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2$ and $\\pm 4.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{3}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{4}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{6}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{12}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{12}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{12}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}, \\\\ \\pm 4, & \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, \\\\ \\pm 6, & \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 12, & \\pm 6, & \\pm 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{3}{4},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 12.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $20$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 10=20.$)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302076 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083232 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a" :knowledge-point/title "Counting the Number of Possible Rational Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65f3eacd-9a6b-543d-b62c-562bfd26d7cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65f3eacd-9a6b-543d-b62c-562bfd26d7cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300476} :answer-field/id #uuid "41a8c4b9-3993-5fd4-a684-936def7a9de9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "94f4999f-cddb-56aa-8dc1-690a88c2e2d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-321645" :question/problem "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=8{x}^{3}-3x+2$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $2,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 2.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $8,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$, $\\pm 2$, $\\pm 4$, and $\\pm 8.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{8}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{2}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{2}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{2}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to their lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1,\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{8},\\text{ } \\\\ \\pm 2,\\text{ }\\pm 1,\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{8},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $10$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 5=10.$)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370028 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083232 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a" :knowledge-point/title "Counting the Number of Possible Rational Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d58f1e42-9acf-5bdd-844b-065909d75d35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d58f1e42-9acf-5bdd-844b-065909d75d35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370030} :answer-field/id #uuid "72a7d874-45df-5575-bf0d-420b6a82e0bb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30c939ec-fbca-5126-a442-cf703484d532" :question/math-academy-id "q-321979" :question/problem "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=-{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-7x+30$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $30,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 3,\\pm 5,\\pm 6,\\pm 10,\\pm 15,\\pm 30.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $-1,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{2}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{3}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{5}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{6}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{10}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{15}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{30}{1}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are only $\\pm 1,$ these simplify to:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\text{ }\\pm 2,\\text{ }\\pm 3,\\text{ }\\pm 5,\\text{ }\\pm 6,\\text{ }\\pm 10,\\text{ }\\pm 15,\\text{ }\\pm 30.\n$$\n\nNotice that none of these potential roots are duplicates. \n\nThis makes a total of $16$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 8=16.$)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433677 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083232 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a" :knowledge-point/title "Counting the Number of Possible Rational Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b174001-a899-57c4-9bb5-2ae14ddc8f25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b174001-a899-57c4-9bb5-2ae14ddc8f25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433641} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f5acde8-ec70-5900-b2f5-c4bcc05cf9e1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e7505886-69c0-558a-a441-b828788cc51e" :question/math-academy-id "q-323225" :question/problem "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=8{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+8$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $8,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$,  $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 4,$ and $\\pm 8.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $8,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$, $\\pm 2$, $\\pm 4,$ and $\\pm 8.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{8}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{2}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{2}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{2}{8}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{4}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{4}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{4}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{4}{8}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{8}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{8}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{8}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{8}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to their lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1,\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{8}, \\\\ \\pm 2,\\text{ }\\pm 1,\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\pm 4,\\text{ }\\pm 2,\\text{ }\\pm 1,\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 8,\\text{ }\\pm 4,\\text{ }\\pm 2,\\text{ }\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{8},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 8.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $14$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 7=14.$)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302144 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "001262b8-82b7-5421-80ac-8608b20d4be1" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a1e8c4f-3bd0-5bf1-8c22-f1c99690ed59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300660}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a1e8c4f-3bd0-5bf1-8c22-f1c99690ed59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300660} :answer-field/id #uuid "87020f36-b7ce-5a27-853f-4f3889feb6e1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301651} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5000c0c0-6878-5962-9058-447048182357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5000c0c0-6878-5962-9058-447048182357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300379} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddb68986-dde5-535c-9d6b-e1fb2db3f1ce" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301932} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca7f8096-1fdc-512c-b5c2-5424359de7fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca7f8096-1fdc-512c-b5c2-5424359de7fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300973} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee9824f4-d39a-5671-af50-6fc79e423711" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301984}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "44f388d6-5078-5e1a-a0fb-9aeba444134a" :question/math-academy-id "q-323610" :question/problem "The polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-10x-8$ has one of its roots in the interval $(3,5).$ In **ascending order**, the distinct real roots of $f(x)$ are\n\n$x=$\n {{field-1}}$,\\,x=$\n {{field-2}}$,\\,x=$\n {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-8$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-8}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 8.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only root in the interval $(3,5)$ is $4.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-10$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $4$ | $12$ | $8$ |\n|  | $1$ | $3$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-4)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-4)({x}^{2}+3x+2).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-4)({x}^{2}+3x+2) \\\\  & =(x-4)(x+1)(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find that, in ascending order, the roots of the polynomial are $x=-2,$ $x=-1,$ and $x=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326676 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "001262b8-82b7-5421-80ac-8608b20d4be1" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21be555d-ccf5-5281-af53-b2f20b2ad18f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324250} {:answer/id #uuid "5ee070d1-a630-540b-987e-0ac74c34b1b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324764} {:answer/id #uuid "8beb16eb-e9fb-5629-a4f3-3b93148cab23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325160} {:answer/id #uuid "af2f4add-ae51-5ca7-9041-e9840321cc19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325461} {:answer/id #uuid "af66a0e6-8e66-5d0f-bc3a-7e64495ed0b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ee070d1-a630-540b-987e-0ac74c34b1b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324764} :answer-field/id #uuid "1386af21-b0ec-52ed-a2ae-90ae0f4d84fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d5d77179-b48c-5827-b197-d34e1d977ab0" :question/math-academy-id "q-38251" :question/problem "Given that the polynomial $f(x)=4{x}^{3}-20{x}^{2}+31x-15$ has one of its roots in the interval $(1,2),$ find the sum of the distinct real roots of $f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-15$ and the leading coefficient is $4.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-15}{\\text{factor of }4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\pm 3,\\pm 5,\\pm 15,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\pm \\frac{5}{2},\\pm \\frac{15}{2},\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\pm \\frac{3}{4},\\pm \\frac{5}{4},\\pm \\frac{15}{4}\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(1,2),$ are $\\frac{3}{2}$ and $\\frac{5}{4}.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-\\frac{3}{2})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $4$ | $-20$ | $31$ | $-15$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $6$ | $-21$ | $15$ |\n|  | $4$ | $-14$ | $10$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-\\frac{3}{2})$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-\\frac{3}{2})(4{x}^{2}-14x+10).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-\\frac{3}{2})(4{x}^{2}-14x+10) \\\\  & =2(x-\\frac{3}{2})(2{x}^{2}-7x+5) \\\\  & =2(x-\\frac{3}{2})(2x-5)(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2},1.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}+1=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335887 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083306 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a75b47ec-8d5a-545e-b915-daca92320322" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3916b332-b1bf-56dd-ba24-217edc46155a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333522} {:answer/id #uuid "69ad4502-e492-59c7-a566-d06801cfd838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333955} {:answer/id #uuid "75a12632-6342-5324-a3b6-e62706c87939" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334051} {:answer/id #uuid "786075ed-ee83-5fb6-a116-f1eab63b1476" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334069} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb95b3c-af6d-5fd3-b35b-7b81469d3cd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69ad4502-e492-59c7-a566-d06801cfd838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333955} :answer-field/id #uuid "6502c1b8-0bfa-5633-9f9c-b5117737a077" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d02cc2f-0a59-5f12-841d-8b0228cd0395" :question/math-academy-id "q-6657" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-2x-1?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-1$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-1}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $2$ does not take the above form. The value $2$ can be interpreted as a fraction $\\frac{2}{1},$ and the numerator $(2)$ is not a factor of $-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395918 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083307 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82ccfef3-569a-5ddf-9a69-cae323a0a312" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f34d47e-e833-592c-bec3-bc39b0793775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395920} {:answer/id #uuid "4c9ab566-ebba-5dfd-86b0-6349eece69a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395921} {:answer/id #uuid "9a355765-0b97-5a42-8de4-b0b60654f204" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395922} {:answer/id #uuid "a0808324-6585-5b82-a886-35c16e8f895c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395923} {:answer/id #uuid "14d0b756