\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-8,$ and the leading coefficient is $5.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-8}{\\text{factor of }5}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-4=-\\frac{4}{1}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(4)$ is a factor of $-8$ and the denominator $(1)$ is a factor of $5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433674 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083306 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a75b47ec-8d5a-545e-b915-daca92320322" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14dbd20f-dae6-536f-87a7-89593f714dee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433636} {:answer/id #uuid "7c44e16a-680d-5060-8d29-321f46d27bfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433647} {:answer/id #uuid "8b19fd5b-b890-57f9-8edb-92c8075ccdef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433649} {:answer/id #uuid "d26b2ae3-d848-5cfd-a798-719a96527d83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433659} {:answer/id #uuid "e42f90f8-5654-57c3-8684-e319bb32b08d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b19fd5b-b890-57f9-8edb-92c8075ccdef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433649} :answer-field/id #uuid "82b73eeb-28f6-544a-b4a5-e943633f0b85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b75db326-1476-547f-a77b-b21ea5825d85" :question/math-academy-id "q-83824" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=5{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}-7x+8?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $8$ and the leading coefficient is $5.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }8}{\\text{factor of }5}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-5$ does not take the above form. The value $-5$ can be interpreted as a fraction $-\\frac{5}{1},$ and the numerator $(5)$ is not a factor of $8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234452 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "001262b8-82b7-5421-80ac-8608b20d4be1" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19199503-6a7e-5f9f-a197-b117e0b7750b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433638} {:answer/id #uuid "b9344a36-f4c2-5ee5-8b24-5bc73e0761f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433652} {:answer/id #uuid "bd738110-2730-510b-a6fe-9f8c39d2690d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433653} {:answer/id #uuid "d02dc8b0-f719-5d72-b3c5-684102a1c360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433657} {:answer/id #uuid "e264283e-cf89-55ac-b653-81b2eaae1082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9344a36-f4c2-5ee5-8b24-5bc73e0761f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433652} :answer-field/id #uuid "479e5fb2-a0db-5547-90a9-3251c9634117" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "38a31983-df9c-53f1-81ab-91f00755216b" :question/math-academy-id "q-83863" :question/problem "Given that the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-10x+8$ has one of its roots in the interval $(-5,-1),$ find the sum of the distinct real roots of $f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $8$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }8}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 4,\\pm 8\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(-5,-1),$ are $-4$ and $-2.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x+4)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-4$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-10$ | $8$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-4$ | $12$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-3$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+4)({x}^{2}-3x+2) \\\\  & =(x+4)(x-1)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=-4,1,2.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n-4+1+2=-1.\n$$"}]}
